Magnesium is needed for healthy bones and for turning food into energy and it also supports muscle development and nerve function (NHS). It is found in a wide variety of foods including spinach, nuts and wholemeal bread, and best sources include wholegrain breakfast cereals such as Cheerios, Shreddies, Shredded Wheat and Weetabix, wholemeal bread, sardines, cheese, white bread and yeast extract (NHS). The reference intake for adults is 300 mg a day for men and 270 mg a day for women aged 19‑64 (NHS). Taking doses above 400 mg may cause diarrhoea (NHS). In the UK, oral magnesium glycerophosphate does not have marketing authorisation for any indication and there are no licensed oral medicines for treating or preventing hypomagnesaemia, with several unlicensed magnesium salts available (NICE).

